+# 0001 — Overdue fines are posted on return, counting open days only
+
+## Context
+
+Late returns were free, so DVDs came back weeks late. We needed a fine rule
+that the desk can explain and that does not change from one day to the next
+while a copy is still out.
+
+## Decision
+
+- A fine is posted to the member's account when the copy is returned, never
+  before. An overdue loan that is still out adds nothing to the balance.
+- Late days are the open days after `due_on`, up to and including the return
+  day. Closed days (`policy.CLOSED_WEEKDAYS`, currently Sunday) are not
+  counted, because nobody can return a copy on them. There is no grace period.
+- Rates per late day: books 0.25 and DVDs 1.00 (`policy.FINE_PER_LATE_DAY`). The fine for
+  one loan is capped at the title's replacement cost.
+- Children are never fined.
+- Each late return posts its own fine. Fines are never merged or recalculated
+  afterwards.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Someone holding an overdue copy is not blocked until they return it. Blocking
+  only reflects posted charges.
+- Changing the rates or the closed days affects only future returns.
+  Fines already posted keep their amounts.
+- Adding accruing fines or lost-item charges later means new kinds of
+  charge, not a change to this rule.
+- Renewals (ADR 0005, planned) add a second point where a fine is posted:
+  renewing an overdue loan posts the fine built up so far, calculated as if
+  the copy had been returned that day.
